#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""whc-theme: render Willow Hayward Code (WHC) colour themes into terminal, editor and desktop configs.

Usage:
  whc-theme list [--devices]                    themes (and machines with a variant)
  whc-theme current                             the theme the dotfiles are rendered with, and this machine's state
  whc-theme apply <theme> [--variant V] [--targets a,b|machine|all] [--dry-run] [--root DIR] [--home DIR]
  whc-theme export <target> [--theme T] [--variant V]   print one target's file(s) to stdout
  whc-theme preview [<theme>] [--variant V]     ANSI colour preview in this terminal
  whc-theme targets                             (alias: --list-targets) every target, where it writes, how it is wired
  whc-theme data-sync --from DIR [--dest DIR]   copy theme data from a whc-theme checkout (or its data/ dir)

Canonical home: github.com/WillowHayward/whc-theme. Theme data comes from the whc-brand repo
(scripts/sync-data there).

Variants: `auto` (default), `base`, or a machine name (cowgirl, bessie, ranch, ...).
  auto  runtime targets (tmux, zsh/p10k/fzf, nvim, lazygit, hyprland) embed every machine and pick
        $WHC_DEVICE (else the short hostname) when they load; shared files in the dotfiles that cannot
        branch (foot, mako, termux) get the device-neutral base; everything else (kitty, Konsole,
        exports...) gets this machine's variant.
  base  device-neutral everywhere.
  NAME  that machine's variant everywhere (runtime targets are pinned to it).

Target groups for --targets: `machine` = this machine's apps outside the dotfiles that are installed here
(gnome, walker, firefox, konsole); `all` = every file target.

Environment: WHC_DEVICE (machine), WHC_DOTFILES_DIR (dotfiles root), WHC_THEME_DATA (data dir),
WHC_THEME_STATE (state file; default ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/whc/theme.toml),
WHC_FIREFOX_PROFILE (a Firefox profile dir; default: every profile in profiles.ini), WHC_GTK_ACCENT
(a libadwaita accent name instead of the theme's nearest).
Python 3.9+ standard library only.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

